114	2	4	Intheinterestofminimizinggreenhousegasemissionsforpowerproduction,environmentalsustainability,energy\ngeneration diversity, and national security, the Department of Energy and the U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission\nanthony.alberti@oregonstate.edu (A.L. Alberti)\nORCID(s):0000-0003-1609-063X (A.L. Alberti)\nAlberti et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 1 of 18\n© 2023 published by Elsevier. This manuscript is made available under the Elsevier user license\nhttps://www.elsevier.com/open-access/userlicense/1.0/Version of Record: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149197022004334\nManuscript_1dbd627b487da91978953a3c65f3aa7d\nAutomation Levels for Nuclear Reactor Operations: A Revised Perspective\n(NRC) have recognized this disadvantage and subsequently implemented a variety of programs to help offset it. The\ngeneralbreadth,impact,andoutcomesoftheseprogramsfallwelloutsidethescopeofthispaper,whichinsteadfocuses\non efforts made to automate reactor operations. Reactor developers, particularly those involved in designing advanced reactors such as small modular reactors\n(SMRs) and microreactors (MRs), have expressed interest in utilizing varying degrees of automation in their designs\nandoperation[17,18].Effortsareunderwaytoexpandtheapplicationofcurrentandfuturenuclearreactorstoinclude\n(1) integration with renewable energy resources so as to achieve nuclear-renewable hybrid systems that enable new\nmarketopportunities[19],(2)flexibleelectricitygeneration(e.g.,usingprocessheattogeneratehydrogenforvehicles\nand industry), (3) nuclear reactor deployment in remote locations affording minimal access, (4) distributed energy\nresources for emergency applications, and (5) net-zero carbon emission [20]. In addition, the Fission Battery (FB)\nInitiativeatIdahoNationalLaboratory[21]hasdefined,focused,andcoordinatedtheresearchanddevelopment(R&D)\nof technologies that can enable battery-like plug-and-play functionality for nuclear energy systems.	1. Int