119	2	9	Automation Approaches\nIntermsofclassifyingtasksforautomationinnuclearpowerplantoperations,O’HaraandHigginsbreakdownthe\ntasks for which reactor operators are responsible into two general types: primary and secondary [23]. Primary tasks,\nwhicharethemainfocusofthiswork,directlyimpacttheoverallfunctionalityandsafetyoftheplant.Secondarytasks\nencompass all other intermediate tasks, such as navigating, arranging, and interrogating information at workstations\nandcontrolpanels[24,23].Followingthehumaninformation-processingconceptspresentedin[25],primarytasksare\nfurther broken down into four sub-classes, with the following examples (adapted from [23]) being provided for each:\n(1) monitoring and detection, (2) situation assessment, (3) response planning, and (4) response implementation. Alberti et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 2 of 18\nAutomation Levels for Nuclear Reactor Operations: A Revised Perspective\nTable 1\nCurrent automation levels published in NUREG-0700. Examples of each are provided in Table 2. Level Human/Machine Interoperability	1. Introduction	106	5	\N	\N	{}	[-0.048332658,-0.06748646,-0.0032308786,0.035325654,-0.008171622,-0.041166417,0.00092609704,0.066734165,-0.106484756,0.031317823,-0.039232533,-0.05261771,0.06322037,0.015085522,-0.032558672,-0.010856145,0.061774455,-0.067176804,-0.07638665,-0.046996754,0.087274484,-0.036218084,0.05064217,0.010607789,-0.06651771,0.008018103,-0.0059164353,-0.06597663,-0.013796166,-0.033577476,-0.022445695,-0.045943365,0.04900167,-0.0041790195,0.027111854,0.026364548,0.018750353,0.027833067,-0.0061435555,-0.07732174,-0.044385444,-0.09984913,-0.03737417,-0.012917805,0.010947444,0.034234956,-0.11375568,-0.16322479,-0.078573376,0.005597791,-0.062241137,4.9301678e-05,0.06331434,0.06083671,0.07200735,0.042346947,0.09046114,-0.027601799,0.029138142,-0.019650195,-0.03738027,-0.028259298,-0.05452195,-0.032109912,0.024773536,0.02870422,0.022061719,-0.023599803,0.028450394,-0.043807313,-0.065235406,-0.02833613,