140	2	30	Active Safety Features and Sustained Operations :\nA key distinction in [37] is the delineation between active safety features, driving automation systems,\nand automated driving. In the context of vehicle operations, active safety systems such as electronic\nstability control, automatic emergency braking, and airbag deployment are excluded from automation,\nas they do not perform driving tasks on a sustained basis, but rather provide momentary intervention\nduring hazardous situations. Since these functions do not necessarily change or eliminate the role of the\ndriver during dynamic driving tasks (DDTs) (i.e., the operational and tactical tasks necessary to operate\na vehicle), they are not classified under automation. Driving automation systems refer to any Level 1–5\nsystemthatperformsallorpartoftheDDTonasustainedbasis(e.g.,adaptivecruisecontroland/orlane\ncentering).ThetermautomateddrivingisreservedforautomationLevels3–5,andreferstothecollective\nsystem (software and hardware) that affords the capability to perform DDTs on a sustained basis. Note\nthat active safety systems and driving automation systems can be components of an automated driving\nsystem (e.g., one comprised of adaptive cruise control, lane centering, navigation, automatic emergency\nbraking, airbags, and electronic stability control [in a driverless taxi]). Per the above concepts, the European Cockpit Association (ECA) and Collins Aerospace each posed analogous\nlevels of automation involving concessions for flight [38, 39].	5 Full Driving	192	6	\N	\N	{}	[-0.036156267,-0.029002715,-0.05708289,-0.02400503,0.047286335,-0.002790181,0.059214626,0.09348933,0.0039180047,0.034036543,0.039647955,-0.015742246,0.073516205,-0.010877236,-0.042369816,-0.03935993,0.06782413,-0.009575571,-0.08828138,0.031833846,0.06742394,0.024784327,0.039443806,0.07273897,-0.13760988,-0.014058913,-0.017004259,-0.05579523,-0.04663277,-0.04281638,-0.051906087,-0.053097293,0.061092667,-0.040607966,-0.02974971,-0.085693