180	2	70	This is especially true when considering\nFBs, as the resilience, reliability, and/or cyber-/physical security requirements involved can widely vary in regard to\nthe deployment arena (e.g., remote mining facilities, defense installations, disaster areas, and data centers). In the\nfollowing sections, we discuss the envisioned needs for enabling AROS features in the context of the aforementioned\nthree primary technologies, their integration into a holistic architecture, and the ensuing regulatory challenges.\n4.1. Sensing\nInthecontextofadvancedautomationanddevelopingtheAROS,thefundamentalroleofsensors(i.e.,toprovide\nreal-time data) is unchanged. Sensor data will still be used to obtain relevant information for general situational\nawareness,planthealthconditions,andtheoperationalstatusofreactorcomponents.Duetotheexpectedcharacteristics\nofnext-generationreactorsystems(i.e.,shrinkingcoresizes[SMRs,MRs,andFBs],easeofinstallation[SMRs,MRs,\nand FBs], and portability [FBs]), installation of a multitude of penetrating sensors and associated data processing\nhardware is expected to be infeasible. Therefore, sensor modalities—the configuration, location, and number of\nsensors—are expected to change. Ratherthandetailpossiblesensorarchitecturesolutionsandconfigurations,weinsteaddiscusstheanticipatedideal\ncharacteristicsofenablingsensortechnologiesforadvancedautomation(i.e.,Levels3,4,and5).Sensorstraditionally\nusedinreactorsystemscanbebroadlycategorizedintotwoclasses:physicalorvirtual.Physicalsensorscanbefurther\nclassifiedaseitheractive(e.g.,neutrondetectors)orpassive(e.g.,fluxwires),andhavealonghistoryofuseinreactor\ncomponents.	5 Full Automation AROS recognizes and diagnoses various alarms, thereby determining that a localized	130	6	\N	\N	{}	[-0.0074265087,-0.07799762,-0.035632644,-0.033601392,0.112881556,-0.0765654,0.051425204,0.06631292,-0.06902709,0.044688255,-0.008008173,-0.054191764,0.0626402,-0.014890526,-0.014953249,0.009911462,0.0880075,-0.0025404035