193	2	83	The interdependence, breadth, and designed operational domain of the above characteristics are expected to be the\nprimary factors in determining the level of automation appropriate for a given component or system. For example, in\nautomationLevels1and2,itcanbeexpectedthatAItechnologieswillbeusedtosupportoperationalstateclassification\nandvisualinspectionsofvariouscomponentsandsubsystems[46],aswellasformaintenanceplanning[47].Here,the\nobjective is to provide operator assistance in day-to-day operations and help alleviate time- and labor-intensive tasks\ntargeting the imperfections that stem from human error and our various cognitive biases [48]. The defining characteristic of an AROS—as well as what effectively enables automation Level 3—is the\nautomation system’s capability to perform sustained tactical and operational tasks. Though potentially limited to a\nspecific operational domain, sustained operations inherently involve maintaining a keen awareness of interconnected\ncomponents and their current and expected modes of operation. This includes (1) the ability to detect and diagnose\nabnormalities and modify operations plans by applying real-time information, and (2) a general self-awareness to\nanomaliesandotherexternalfactors,aswellastheabilitytoreactbasedonunderstandingthesystemstate,theresources\navailable, and what responses are appropriate. In other relevant safety-critical industries (e.g., self-driving road-\ngoing vehicles), this behavior is typically accomplished via learning-based control and multi-objective optimization\n[49, 50, 51].	5 Full Automation AROS recognizes and diagnoses various alarms, thereby determining that a localized	173	6	\N	\N	{}	[0.013233148,-0.03578757,-0.022291476,0.02262753,-0.022224287,-0.047650643,0.09511734,0.05611074,-0.07370311,0.07036387,-0.03366605,-0.01637815,0.03572653,0.044665415,-0.015464071,-0.028238365,0.094239965,-0.06351403,-0.13066806,-0.01490123,0.115575336,0.011533436,0.008874902,0.064160995,-0.1031