206	2	96	Each\nentail their own set of unique challenges to be considered. Development of an AROS presents particular challenges regarding operations licensing and regulating, as well as\nensuringgeneralhumanreadiness.Astechnologyisdevelopedandprogressesfromonetechnologyreadinesslevelto\nthe next, the role (and readiness or compatibility) of the human operator (or supervisor) must be considered through\nbasicR&D,technologydemonstrations,andfull-scaletestingandqualification[67].Asecondkeyregulatoryhurdleis\nthedevelopmentandapprovaloftheplanthumanfactorsengineeringprogram[68,69].Thisprogramiswide-reaching\nand aims to ensure general plant safety throughout the plant’s lifetime. Its current tenants should be embraced at any\nand all of the six proposed automation levels. Crossing the proposed automation tipping point will be particularly\nchallenging,asthisiswheretraditionalreactorcontrolparadigmsshiftfromhuman-tomachine-centric,andmaylead\nto an evolution in how control room designs, operations procedures, and operator training/education are measured. Alberti et al.: Preprint submitted to Elsevier Page 14 of 18\nAutomation Levels for Nuclear Reactor Operations: A Revised Perspective\n5. Future Work and Conclusions\nTo provide a unified framework for future R&D, this work updates the guidelines pertaining to the currently\nacceptedlevelsofautomationforreactoroperations.Thenewlyproposedlevelsbuilduponthecurrentguidelinesbyen-\nablingnext-generationnuclearreactortechnologytobecomeincreasinglyeconomicallycompetitiveandcommercially\nviable relative to competing power generation sources.	5 Full Automation AROS recognizes and diagnoses various alarms, thereby determining that a localized	150	6	\N	\N	{}	[-0.04468654,-0.061621353,-0.037036587,0.002442199,-0.039304838,-0.022513958,-0.02835049,0.06150425,-0.11415426,0.053276546,0.04484062,-0.07038395,0.063277915,0.010828083,-0.06830813,0.0035014434,0.0047609396,-0.033855323,-0.068305805,-0.024616601,0.0818494,-0.00038118084,0.0