498	5	15	The results suggest\nthat people tend to follow AI suggestions with limited critical evaluation, often accepting them without thoroughly\nprocessing the outcomes. Users show a tendency to rely on AI suggestions, even when the AI is wrong. Such\nover-reliance can be explained by cognitive bias, where users may perceive the AI system as an authoritative figure,\nespecially in tasks requiring complex decision-making. When AI is correct, users demonstrate a greater degree of trust, suggesting that accurate AI assistance can reinforce\nuser confidence in the system. However, when AI is incorrect, the lack of significant correction by the users indicates\nEngaging with AI\nAI is Incorrect Correct (%) Incorrect (%) p-value\nCorrect 109 (22.4%) 94 (19.3%) < 0.001\nIncorrect 19 (3.9%) 264 (54.3%)\nAI is Correct Correct (%) Incorrect (%) p-value\nCorrect 1131 (67.6%) 62 (3.7%) < 0.001\nIncorrect 200 (11.9%) 281 (16.8%)\nTable 1: McNemar Test Results for User Decisions with AI\nSuggestions.\nCorrectPhase 1 Correctness\nIncorrectCorrectPhase 2 Correctness\nIncorrect\nAI Suggestion is Correct\nAI Suggestion is Incorrect Figure 2: Visualization of User Answer Correctness.\npotential risks in over-reliance.	2.1 Users Over-Rely on AI Suggestions, Even When Incorrect (H1)	179	5	\N	\N	{}	[0.037948996,-0.067041695,0.016080601,0.014187884,-0.03462765,0.016327647,0.06435915,0.15937074,-0.0041445633,0.05138938,-0.028041432,-0.011635167,0.082100004,0.027847711,-0.02519852,-0.037564103,0.10135259,-0.008377253,-0.043033265,-0.06695702,0.011496817,0.012500015,0.013621332,-0.051331207,-0.074910924,-0.022869544,-0.051489826,-0.034013823,-0.052653473,-0.026398858,-0.0051454892,0.03643441,0.03702809,-0.011037227,-0.10093853,-0.013552528,-0.10775736,0.09061728,0.03758572,-0.032033585,-0.062041268,-0.03661716,0.08048783,0.001128528,0.061682686,0.0068153804,-0.06907582,0.013175199,-0.027233956,0.043457773,-0.12036175,0.018389523,0.046248354,0.012100921,-0.047897987,0.014041797,0.038012832,