1136	9	0	Safety Science\nVolume 169, January 2024, 106329\nSupporting human supervision in autonomous\ncollision avoidance through agent transparency\nKoen van de Merwe , Steven Mallam , Salman Nazir , Øystein Engelhardtsen\nShow more\nOutline\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.ssci.2023.106329\nGet rights and content\nUnder a Creative Commons license Open access\nHighlights\n•Autonomous ships expected to adopt artificially intelligent agents for\nnavigation.\n•Human supervision anticipated to assure safe performance, but\nsupport needed.\n•Requirements for agent information disclosure defined using cognitive\ntask analysis.\n•Insight into agent internal reasoning gives understandability and\npredictability.\n•Affording humans with agent transparency supports safe and effective\nsupervision. Abstractab bc b a\nShare Cite12/20/25, 2:05 PM Supporting human supervision in autonomous collision avoidance through agent transparency - ScienceDirect\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0925753523002710 1/37\nPrevious NextOngoing trends in society point towards the adoption of intelligent agents across safety critical\nindustries. In the maritime domain, artificially intelligent agents may soon be capable of\nautonomously performing collision and grounding avoidance (CAGA); a task traditionally\nperformed by humans. Consequently, the role of humans is anticipated to change from those\nperforming collision avoidance to those supervising an agent performing collision avoidance. One of\nthe key concerns with regards to human factors is avoiding the out-of-the-loop performance\nproblem where hu mans lose situation awareness (SA) and become susceptible to misinterpreting\nthe agent’s decisions and planned actions.	Preamble	214	5	\N	\N	{}	[0.05805037,-0.011674731,-0.02752572,0.05166615,0.08029002,-0.04184381,0.08054236,0.02061905,0.014507603,0.0776169,0.036468517,-0.04389402,-0.0017261114,0.048960745,-0.0063268384,-0.03798803,0.0890399,0.027881863,-0.14825761,0.004490046,0.