1411	11	14	Based on\nthese levels, Cho [15] defines a shared ODD management\nresponsibility table, up to which level the human operator is\nrequired to oversee the state of the ODD.The concept of ODD is also increasingly being adopted in\nother domains, such as the shipping industry [16], Unmanned\nAerial Vehicles [17], and the train industry [18]. According\nto Sun et al. [19], ODDs are particularly useful in several key\ntasks:\n•Design Process: Describing the ODD helps identify\nrelevant scenarios the autonomous system must handle,\nallowing developers to define both high- and low-level\nsystem requirements.\n•Verification and Validation: The ODD can be used to\ngenerate test cases for simulation-based testing, ensuring\nthe system can handle its intended operational conditions.\n•Real-time Monitoring: During operation, ODD moni-\ntoring can be utilized to measure and validate system\nperformance in real time. In EASAs concept paper [3] building blocks such as AI\nassurance, Human Factors for AI, and AI risk mitigation are\ndefined for the development of trustworthy AI.	Preamble	163	3	\N	\N	{}	[-0.09325287,-0.029355451,-0.040363908,0.012229809,0.031265486,-0.108087294,0.025489613,0.038306817,-0.07768634,0.07652846,0.051672976,-0.058662597,-0.0024281428,0.012987344,-0.051162537,0.0070921583,0.023630863,-0.06954391,-0.07233987,-0.008972105,0.035166204,-0.074028134,-0.062174324,-0.023358034,-0.13477983,0.0010501632,0.0006823479,-0.018639479,0.00950345,-0.08242042,0.03022744,0.014970168,0.08889829,-0.04425414,-0.0055793705,-3.8382674e-05,0.023048317,-0.041506875,0.047412593,-0.03284253,-0.051324356,-0.10894403,-0.0012107613,-0.028141791,-0.0021116424,0.00050590024,0.028761245,-0.044404436,-0.023136633,0.020011885,-0.017035518,-0.005005792,0.0011365275,-0.035186443,-0.029297715,0.038586225,0.04329122,-0.03661009,-0.012225506,-0.006456211,-0.058653653,0.05829829,-0.050584927,-0.012122364,0.05408398,0.013529591,-0.07043935,-0.015435368,-0.03395512,0.0022749489,0.0291514