1440	11	43	Figure 3: DIRC functionality detects a conflict, finds a\nresolution proposal, and displays a solution in the controller\nworking position to get the human ATCO’s feedback on the\nsolution.\n1) Trajectory Conflict Checker: Within the DIRC\nworkspace, a flight trajectory is defined as a sequence of\nsegments between waypoints where a change of speed, ∆v,\nor flight level is assumed to be linear. Thus, each position on\na segment vector ⃗ scan be calculated by\npnew=porg+\nv+∆v\n2·t\n·t·⃗ sdir\n⃗ slength, (1)\nwhere vdenotes the speed, tthe time and ⃗ sdirthe direction\nvector of ⃗ s. For conflict detection, the Trajectory Conflict\nChecker tool (TCC) was implemented based on this linear\nmovement equation. Using Equation (1)the distance at time t\nbetween flights on segments ⃗ s1and ⃗ s2can be formulated and\nthe minimal distance for this link combination is calculated by\nsolving the differential quotient of this formula in dependence\noft. Simple cases such as “no speed change” are solved\nbeforehand. In the case of imaginary solutions for this\ncalculation, a step-by-step calculation is used instead.	Preamble	177	6	\N	\N	{}	[-0.05079357,-0.049440842,-0.10644685,-0.065948114,0.046232976,0.0007592283,-0.012852049,0.010794174,0.06554997,0.05390788,0.070121914,0.02956745,-0.01148882,-0.0051550814,-0.08821027,-0.024408136,-0.011211192,0.01951865,-0.008411791,0.0013812913,0.046041384,0.011132717,-0.009210406,0.034379326,-0.09157697,0.013813153,0.0030983214,-0.041405573,-0.052819386,-0.05095661,0.08327102,0.059427362,0.0058030514,0.0280778,-0.053063106,-0.033819884,0.008321051,-0.00021230037,0.011506983,-0.08501996,-0.053607695,0.024853673,0.00989087,-0.023895575,0.011251918,0.028989973,-0.05963838,0.053652983,-0.039472293,0.02121991,-0.057380375,0.01734474,-0.0007058821,-0.05824728,0.093619205,0.06632274,0.03312039,0.057249077,0.029715199,-0.008372416,0.008045792,-0.026714122,-0.041827623,-0.036524583,0.05527934,-0.01981658,0.0046796775,0.031276904,0.04729814