1487	12	0	ETH Library\nUnmanned and Autonomous\nSystems: Future of Automation in\nProcess and Energy Industries\nConference Paper\nAuthor(s):\nBorghesan, Francesco; Zagorowska, Marta; Mercangöz, Mehmet\nPublication date:\nPermanent link:\nhttps://doi.org/https://doi.org/10.3929/ethz-b-000583301\nRights / license:\nCreative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International\nOriginally published in:\nIFAC-PapersOnLine 55(7), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ifacol.2022.07.555\nThis page was generated automatically upon download from the ETH Zurich Research Collection . For more information, please consult the Terms of use . IFAC PapersOnLine 55-7 (2022) 875–882\nScienceDirect\nScienceDirectAvailable online at www.sciencedirect.com\n2405-8963 Copyright © 2022 The Authors. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license . Peer review under responsibility of International Federation of Automatic Control.\n10.1016/j.ifacol.2022.07.555\n10.1016/j.ifacol.2022.07.555 2405-8963Copyright © 2022 The Authors. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license\n(https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ )Unmanned and Autonomous Systems: Future of Automation in Process and\nEnergy Industries\nFrancesco Borghesan * Marta Zagorowska* Mehmet Mercangöz *\n* Department of Chemical Engineering, Imperial College London ,\nSouth Kensington Campus, London SW7 2AZ, UK (m.mercangoz@imperial.ac.uk )\nAbstract : Process and energy industries have been recognised as adopters of high levels of automation\ncompared to other sectors. Nonetheless, human cognitive input still plays a critical role in the operation of\nprocess plants and replication of these cognitive capabilities remains a key challenge for advancing\nautomation levels. In this paper, w e provide an analysis of process and energy industries based on a scenario\nof reduced availability of skilled labour and increased demands for safety, sustainability, and resilience.	Preamble	241	6	\N	\N	{}	[